        <p>
          Say "name picker wheel" and most people picture a teacher calling on
          students — a screen-based stand-in for pulling a stick out of a jar.
          That's a real use, and it works well. But it's also just the entry
          point for what this tool can actually do.
        </p>
        <p>
          In offices, the exact same mechanism runs duty rosters, rotates who
          leads the standup, randomises retro formats, and breaks genuine ties
          without putting any one person in the position of having chosen. In
          classrooms, it hands out peer review partners, decides presentation
          order, and builds project groups in a way students experience as fair
          rather than quietly rigged.
        </p>
        <p>
          What ties all of this together is one effect:
          <strong
            >a decision that comes from a wheel gets accepted in a way the same
            decision, made by a person, often doesn't.</strong
          >
          Below are ten uses worth trying, each with a concrete setup so you can
          put it into practice today.
        </p>

