        <!-- ── WHY IT WORKS ── -->
        <h2 id="why-name-wheel">Why This Simple Tool Actually Works</h2>

        <p>
          The name picker wheel's real strength isn't fairness in the abstract —
          it's <strong>how fair the process looks</strong> to the people
          standing around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a teacher keeps calling on the same handful of students — even
          with the best intentions, even just because those hands go up first —
          the students left out notice. They build a story about who gets
          favoured, and that story sticks around long after it stops being true.
          Meetings have the same pattern: the same person always takes notes,
          the same person always runs the call, the same voice always gets heard
          first. Nobody says it out loud, but everyone feels the two-tier
          dynamic building underneath.
        </p>
        <p>
          Take a person out of the selection step entirely, and the fairness
          stops being something you have to take on trust — it becomes something
          the whole room can watch happen. That visibility is the actual
          mechanism at work here, not just a nice side effect.
        </p>

        <div class="stat-strip">
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">Fewer</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >student objections to being called on when selection moves from
              hand-raising to a visible random wheel, according to teacher
              reports</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">More</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >unique contributors per meeting once a random facilitator
              rotation replaces a fixed lead or volunteer system</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="stat-box">
            <span class="stat-num">0</span>
            <span class="stat-label"
              >disputes about who "always" gets picked once the process is
              visibly random rather than person-driven</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 1 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case school" id="use1">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">1</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Calling on Students Without the Dread</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-school">🏫 Classroom</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              The classic lolly-stick draw — names on sticks, pulled from a pot
              — gets a real upgrade from a wheel: everyone in the room watches
              it happen in real time, nobody can quietly slip a stick back in,
              and there's a genuine moment of shared suspense as it slows down.
            </p>
            <p>
              Getting the anxiety level right comes down to how it's framed and
              how consistently it's used. Students who know <em>anyone</em>
              could be picked — not just the ones with their hands up — start
              preparing differently. But that only pays off if the wheel is a
              routine part of class, not a surprise tool for catching people
              out. Set the tone early: "everyone gets a turn," not "you never
              know when I'll catch you."
            </p>
            <p>
              For anxious or quieter students, a simple rule helps a lot: give
              it your best shot, pass to a classmate, or say "give me a second"
              and circle back before the end of class. The wheel picks — the
              student still decides how to respond. That balance keeps the
              fairness benefit without adding unnecessary pressure.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block school">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎓 Setup: load first names, switch on Remove After Spin so
                everyone gets a turn before anyone repeats, and reset it at the
                start of each week or unit.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 2 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case work" id="use2">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">2</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Splitting Up the Jobs Nobody Wants</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-work">💼 Workplace</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Every team has its unloved tasks: writing up meeting notes,
              booking the room, taking the first support ticket of the morning.
              When those jobs quietly land on the same one or two people again
              and again — even without anyone deciding it that way — resentment
              builds slowly and unevenly.
            </p>
            <p>
              Handing the decision to a wheel takes it out of anyone's hands.
              Load the team and spin at the start of the week, sprint, or
              meeting, whatever fits the task. Whoever gets picked has a job
              that's hard to argue with — a wheel chose them, not a manager, not
              a colleague dodging it.
            </p>
            <p>
              There's a bonus here too: it sometimes hands a task to someone who
              wouldn't normally take it on, which builds cross-team familiarity,
              spreads out institutional knowledge, and stops any one person from
              becoming the sole expert in something small but important.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block work">
              <div class="seg-label">
                💼 Example segment sets for common workplace duty wheels
              </div>
              <div class="pill-list">
                <span class="pill pill-blue">📝 Meeting notes</span>
                <span class="pill pill-blue">📅 Books the room</span>
                <span class="pill pill-blue">☕ Organises team lunch</span>
                <span class="pill pill-blue"
                  >📣 Presents the sprint update</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-blue">🔍 First code review today</span>
                <span class="pill pill-blue">📬 Checks the shared inbox</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 3 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case school" id="use3">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">3</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Group Assignments Nobody Can Argue With</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-school">🏫 Classroom</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Group work assignment is one of the most sensitive moments in any
              classroom. Free choice recreates the same friendship clusters
              every time and leaves someone out. Teacher-picked groups get
              challenged too: "you did that on purpose," or "she always gets the
              good group." A wheel sidesteps both problems entirely.
            </p>
            <p>
              The cleanest method uses two wheels in sequence: one with student
              names, one with group labels — Group A, Group B, Group C. Spin the
              name wheel to pick a student, spin the group wheel to assign them,
              repeat until everyone's placed. Since both steps are visibly
              random, no student has real grounds to argue the grouping was set
              up against them.
            </p>
            <p>
              Turn on remove-after-spin for the name wheel so each student lands
              exactly once. If numbers come out slightly uneven, spin again to
              reassign the last name — keeping even that fix visibly random
              matters.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block school">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎓 Tip: for groups working together over several weeks, consider
                sticking with the wheel's assignment even if it looks imperfect
                at first. Groups that wouldn't have chosen each other often
                produce stronger, more varied work over time.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 4 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case work" id="use4">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">4</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Rotating Who Runs the Meeting</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-work">💼 Workplace</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Most meetings get run by the same person every time — usually
              whoever's most senior, or whoever sent the invite. Over time, that
              person builds strong facilitation instincts while everyone else
              stays passive. It also means the meeting keeps reflecting one
              person's priorities and style, which can slowly narrow what the
              team actually talks about.
            </p>
            <p>
              A facilitator wheel breaks that pattern. Spin it at the end of
              each session to pick who runs the next one. That person owns the
              agenda, keeps time, and decides how to handle disagreements. For
              teams where some members have never run a meeting before, this
              builds facilitation skills faster than almost anything else — and
              it changes how those people show up as attendees, too.
            </p>
            <p>
              For larger department meetings, a lighter version works well: spin
              to shortlist three candidates, then let them sort out who runs it.
              It keeps some flexibility while the selection stays visibly random
              rather than top-down.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block work">
              <div class="seg-label">
                💼 Load the full team, remove whoever led last cycle so no one
                gets picked twice in a row, and reset the wheel every quarter or
                half-year.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 5 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case both" id="use5">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">5</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Pairing People Up for Honest Feedback</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-both">🏫💼 Both</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Peer review is genuinely valuable — right up until people choose
              their own reviewers. Self-selected feedback tends to cluster
              around friendship rather than honesty: reviewers soften their
              notes, and recipients know it. The whole exercise loses most of
              its point.
            </p>
            <p>
              A wheel assigns review partners with no regard for who's close to
              whom, which tends to produce more direct feedback from reviewers
              with nothing personal at stake, and recipients who read the
              pairing as fair rather than targeted. That neutrality gives both
              sides cover — the reviewer can be blunt because "the wheel put us
              together," and the recipient takes it as impartial, not personal.
            </p>
            <p>
              In classrooms, spin to pair students one-to-one or in threes. At
              work, spin to decide who reviews whose code, writing, or design
              each cycle. Keeping a simple log of past pairings helps spread
              review relationships across the whole team over time instead of
              settling into the same informal pairs.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎯 Pairs vs. triads: one-to-one is quicker; three-person groups
                give richer feedback but take longer. Use pairs for everyday
                work, triads for anything higher-stakes.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 6 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case work" id="use6">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">6</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Keeping Retrospectives Fresh</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-work"
                  >💼 Workplace — Agile Teams</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Sprint retros fall into a predictable rhythm fast enough that
              teams often stop really thinking and start giving ritual answers:
              "what went well — communication; what didn't — estimation; what to
              change — better docs." Same format, same answers, sprint after
              sprint.
            </p>
            <p>
              A retro-format wheel breaks the habit by picking the structure at
              random instead of defaulting to "start/stop/continue" every time.
              It might land on "five whys," the sailboat retro, or "one word per
              person." Different formats surface different things from the same
              team, and nobody can walk in with a rehearsed answer because they
              don't know the format in advance.
            </p>
            <p>
              A second wheel can pick who presents each retro category, which
              spreads out the summarising work and stops one person from always
              framing the team's conclusions. This works particularly well for
              remote teams, where the same visible voice tends to dominate async
              discussion.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block work">
              <div class="seg-label">💼 Sample retro-format wheel segments</div>
              <div class="pill-list">
                <span class="pill pill-blue">⛵ Sailboat retro</span>
                <span class="pill pill-indigo"
                  >🤔 Five whys on top blocker</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-blue">🟢 Start / Stop / Continue</span>
                <span class="pill pill-indigo"
                  >💬 One word per person, then discuss</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-blue">⭐ Rose / Bud / Thorn</span>
                <span class="pill pill-indigo"
                  >❤️ What made you proud this sprint?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-blue">🎯 Mad / Sad / Glad</span>
                <span class="pill pill-indigo"
                  >🧩 Team health check — 4 questions</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 7 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case school" id="use7">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">7</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Deciding Who Reads or Presents First</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-school">🏫 Classroom</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Reading aloud and presentation order share a familiar problem:
              going first is nerve-wracking, going last gives extra prep time,
              and students who already know their slot tend to check out until
              it's their turn. Volunteer-based systems favour confident
              students; strict alphabetical order sentences the same few
              students to go first every single time.
            </p>
            <p>
              A wheel keeps the next reader genuinely unknown until it lands.
              Students who might be called at any moment track the text more
              closely — they need to know where the class is, not just where
              their own paragraph starts. That passive attention shift alone is
              often reason enough for literacy teachers to switch.
            </p>
            <p>
              For presentation order, spin it right when presentations begin —
              not days ahead. That stops early presenters from checking out once
              they're done and stops late-slot students from endlessly tweaking
              their work. The randomness itself is the structure.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block school">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎓 For reading aloud, leave remove-after-spin off so the same
                student could be called again — that keeps attention up
                throughout the reading, not just until each student's first
                turn.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 8 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case both" id="use8">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">8</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Icebreakers That Don't Fall Flat</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-both">🏫💼 Both</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Icebreakers usually die the same way: the facilitator asks "does
              anyone want to share?" and the same three people answer while
              everyone else waits it out. Round-the-room is a bit better but
              still awkward — people either rehearse an answer or freeze when
              it's their turn.
            </p>
            <p>
              A two-wheel setup fixes both problems at once: one wheel with
              names, one with question prompts. Spin the name wheel to pick a
              speaker, spin the question wheel to pick what they answer. The
              person picked gets a brief moment of attention, but on a question
              the wheel chose — not one that feels pointed at them.
            </p>
            <p>
              This works especially well for the first five minutes of a weekly
              standup, the start of a new term, onboarding sessions, or the
              opening of any workshop where the group doesn't know each other
              well yet. It spreads out speaking time naturally and creates an
              energy shift that static round-the-room intros just don't.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎯 Sample icebreaker question wheel — works for classrooms and
                offices alike
              </div>
              <div class="pill-list">
                <span class="pill pill-teal"
                  >🌍 Furthest place you've ever been?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-green"
                  >📚 Last book you couldn't put down?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-teal"
                  >🎵 Song stuck in your head this week?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-orange"
                  >🍕 Unpopular food opinion you'll defend?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-teal"
                  >🧩 Skill you'd learn if time wasn't a factor?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-green"
                  >😂 Last thing that made you laugh out loud?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-teal"
                  >🌅 Morning person or night owl — explain?</span
                >
                <span class="pill pill-orange"
                  >🏆 Something you're quietly proud of?</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 9 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case both" id="use9">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">9</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>A Prize Draw Nobody Can Dispute</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-both">🏫💼 Both</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              Prize draws share the same weak point everywhere: whoever runs the
              draw is quietly suspected of tipping the outcome, even when they
              had nothing to do with it. A teacher with a child in the class who
              runs a hat draw will get raised eyebrows no matter who wins. A
              manager who hand-picks a name for a team prize faces the same
              suspicion.
            </p>
            <p>
              Running the draw live on a wheel — projected on screen, every
              entry visible before the first spin — turns fairness from
              something people have to trust into something they can just see.
              Show the full list, display the wheel to the room, have someone
              other than the organiser click spin, and record the result. For
              school settings, our detailed walkthrough on
              <a href="/blogs/school-raffle-spin-wheel" class="ilink"
                >running a school raffle using a spin wheel</a
              >
              covers the whole process.
            </p>
            <p>
              The same idea applies at work for any recognition draw, raffle, or
              end-of-year bonus spin. Record it and share it with the team
              afterward — nobody can dispute something they watched happen and
              can replay on demand.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎯 Always enable Remove After Spin for prize draws, always show
                the full entry list beforehand, and always have someone other
                than the organiser click spin.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ USE 10 ═══ -->
        <div class="use-case both" id="use10">
          <div class="uc-header">
            <div class="uc-number">10</div>
            <div class="uc-title-block">
              <h3>Settling a Genuine Toss-Up Decision</h3>
              <div class="uc-subtitle">
                <span class="ctx-badge ctx-both">🏫💼 Both</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="uc-body">
            <p>
              This one's easy to overlook, but it's genuinely useful.
              Committees, teacher panels, and hiring groups sometimes land on a
              true deadlock — two candidates, two projects, two approaches that
              come out equal on every measure that matters. At that point, more
              deliberation usually just loops the same conversation without
              turning up anything new.
            </p>
            <p>
              A wheel loaded with the two options makes a legitimate, defensible
              tie-breaker. It takes the decision out of any one person's hands,
              so nobody on the committee carries the weight — or the social cost
              — of having chosen. The process stays fair, documented, and hard
              to second-guess afterward.
            </p>
            <p>
              One thing worth watching: the moment the result lands, notice your
              own reaction. If it lands on Option A and someone feels genuinely
              let down, that's a sign the group wasn't as evenly split as the
              discussion suggested. Treat that reaction as useful information —
              it can surface a preference the formal evaluation never quite
              captured. We dig into this more in our guide on
              <a href="/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use" class="ilink"
                >when a Yes or No Wheel actually helps</a
              >.
            </p>
            <div class="seg-block">
              <div class="seg-label">
                🎯 For anything high-stakes, agree the commitment upfront:
                "whatever this spin lands on, we act on it." The commitment
                matters more than which option wins.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

