        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does a name picker wheel give every name a genuinely equal
            chance?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes, as long as every segment takes up equal arc space. The result
            comes from a random number generated the instant the spin starts.
            With 30 students loaded, each one has a 1-in-30 chance on any given
            spin — unaffected by recent picks, where names sit on the wheel, or
            anything else. For the full technical explanation, see our article
            on
            <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink"
              >whether a digital spin wheel is truly random</a
            >.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            How should I handle an anxious student who gets picked?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Set a clear, consistent rule before you start using the wheel for
            cold-calling: a student can pass once per class, or say "give me a
            second" and come back to it before class ends. Announce this on day
            one so it's a known option, not something improvised on the spot.
            The point of the wheel is fair distribution of participation — not
            stripping away student choice. Students who know the pass option
            exists tend to use it less, because knowing it's there lowers the
            anxiety of being picked in the first place.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can I keep separate name lists for different classes or teams?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — the wheel's URL stores your exact setup. Build one wheel per
            class or team, bookmark each one by name, and load the right one
            each session. You can keep as many saved configurations as you need
            — Year 9 English, Marketing Team, Board Meeting — each with its own
            list and settings. Switching between them is as quick as switching
            browser tabs.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Is there a limit to how many names I can add?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Up to 500 names fit cleanly on a single wheel—enough for any class, club, or mid-sized raffle. With hundreds of entries, slices narrow visually, while cryptographic selection and remove-after-spin stay mathematically uniform. On classroom projectors, 20 to 35 names read cleanly from the back row.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can someone game a name picker wheel by clicking at the right
            moment?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            No — the outcome comes directly from the browser's Web Cryptography API (<code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) with rejection sampling the instant the spin fires, so click timing has zero effect on where the pointer stops. Transparency at setup (showing the full list before spinning) is what prevents disputes.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can I weight the wheel so certain names come up more often?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — click the <code>+</code> or <code>-</code> weight buttons (1x to 20x) beside any name to scale its odds without duplicating labels, or switch on duplicate entries to add multiple rows. At 2x, a student earning bonus participation credit gets double the slice share cleanly in front of the group.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does it work for remote or hybrid meetings?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — share your screen during the call so everyone can watch the
            spin live. It runs in any browser with nothing to install. In hybrid
            meetings, screen-sharing the wheel on the video call gives remote
            participants the exact same view as everyone in the room, so the
            selection moment is genuinely shared rather than something only
            in-person attendees witnessed.
          </div>
        </div>


